### VS Code ###
.vscode/

# AspectJ weaver crash dumps (see the aspectjweaver javaagent in pom.xml)
ajcore.*.txt

# Allure reports, one self-contained HTML file per run (see gmavenplus-plugin in pom.xml)
/reports/

# Worktrees an agent session creates for isolated work. Each one is a full checkout of this
# repository, so committing it would nest the repo inside itself, and `git add -A` will
# otherwise sweep one in. Remove a stale one with `git worktree remove`.
/.claude/worktrees/

# Per-machine operator settings for the "local" profile, layered over application.yaml by the
# "Local SpringApp" run configuration. It names one person's archive -- a path off their own
# machine -- so it never belongs in a commit. It sits with the other resources so Spring resolves
# it from the classpath rather than the working directory; that does mean a packaged jar carries a
# copy, which is why this entry is the only thing keeping the path out of the repository. Ignoring
# it does not unstage it, so check `git status` if it was ever added before this line existed.
/src/main/resources/application-local.yaml

# The working directory a run writes to: vespera.db and the operator's profile.yaml. The default is
# ${user.dir}/.vespera (see vespera.working-dir in application.yaml), so a run started from the repo
# root leaves it here. It is a real corpus's ledger -- file paths, hashes and verdicts from someone's
# own archive -- and it is per-machine state besides, so it never belongs in a commit.
/.vespera/

# Agent tooling's own per-session state, written beside the checked-in agent configuration rather
# than under it: the context-compaction bookkeeping a Claude Code session keeps, and Serena's
# language-server cache and project file. All of it is regenerated on demand and describes one
# machine's session, not this repository's work.
/.claude/.headroom_wrap_*.json
/.claude/.headroom_wrap_settings.lock
/.serena/

# A second agent tool's copy of the same installed skills, written at the repository root rather than
# beside the first's, and the lockfile the installer writes next to it. Both are an install of
# something published elsewhere, restored by re-running the installer, and both describe one
# machine's choice of tooling rather than this repository's work. The lockfile pins the skills that
# machine installed, not a dependency this project builds against -- nothing in the pom, the build or
# any workflow reads it -- so checking it in would state a version this repository does not hold.
/.opencode/
/skills-lock.json

# The symlinks that hand those same installed skills to Claude Code, one per skill, pointing into the
# install above. They are per-machine for the same reason it is, and they break the moment the
# install moves: 25 of them pointed at a `.agents/skills/` directory that no longer existed, which
# git reported as 25 warnings on every `git status` rather than as anything it could act on. The rest
# of `.claude/` is checked in deliberately — the agents, the settings and the workflows are this
# repository's, and only this one directory beneath it is an install.
/.claude/skills/

# Qodana's local run: the SARIF report it writes at the repository root (megabytes of it, one file
# per run), its cache directory, and the profile the IDE generated to produce them. All three are
# IDE-local today -- nothing in .github/workflows runs Qodana -- and `git add -A` sweeps them in
# otherwise. If Qodana ever joins CI, qodana.yaml is the one of the three that belongs in the
# repository, and this entry loses its last line.
/qodana.sarif.json
/.qodana/
/qodana.yaml
